Title: Diagnosis

Details: 
The failing test `testAddNaN` checks the behavior of the `add` method in the `Complex` class when one of the operands is NaN (Not a Number). The contract in the javadoc states that if either complex number (this or rhs) has a NaN part, then Complex.NaN should be returned.

Upon inspecting the `add` method implementation, it simply checks if the argument `rhs` is null and then returns a new Complex instance by summing corresponding real and imaginary parts without any special handling for NaN:

```java
public Complex add(Complex rhs) throws NullArgumentException {
    MathUtils.checkNotNull(rhs);
    return createComplex(real + rhs.getReal(), imaginary + rhs.getImaginary());
}
```

In contrast, the `subtract` method explicitly checks if either operand is NaN:

```java
public Complex subtract(Complex rhs) throws NullArgumentException {
    MathUtils.checkNotNull(rhs);
    if (isNaN || rhs.isNaN) {
        return NaN;
    }
    return createComplex(real - rhs.getReal(), imaginary - rhs.getImaginary());
}
```

The constructor for `Complex` sets the `isNaN` flag if either the real or imaginary parts are NaN:

```java
public Complex(double real, double imaginary) {
    this.real = real;
    this.imaginary = imaginary;
    isNaN = Double.isNaN(real) || Double.isNaN(imaginary);
    ...
}
```

The field `NaN` is a static final Complex with both parts as Double.NaN.

Since the `add` method does not check and return Complex.NaN if either operand is NaN, but directly creates a new Complex from the sums (and sums involving NaN will propagate NaN naturally in Double arithmetic), calling `add` with Complex.NaN does not produce a Complex instance with the `isNaN` flag set to true. Thus, `z.isNaN()` in the test fails.

This discrepancy between `add` and `subtract` violates the javadoc contract, which states both should return Complex.NaN if either operand has NaN parts.

Hence, the root cause is that `add` method does not perform an explicit check for NaN operands and return the static `Complex.NaN`, unlike `subtract`.

The recent partial bug reports suggesting the javadoc contract and NaN handling inconsistencies further support this diagnosis.

Suggested fix: The `add` method should be modified to check if either operand is NaN and return `Complex.NaN` accordingly, aligning with the contract and behavior of `subtract`.

